  ('e7220798-c856-59b2-948e-f93a8a4aca8c','fa83069c-7a21-5f41-b8a7-e4d3b724a2e0',1,'B','τ = p × E (cross product)'),
  ('0426fb04-8bc9-5309-849d-ffcb23622d24','fa83069c-7a21-5f41-b8a7-e4d3b724a2e0',1,'C','τ = p + E (vector sum)'),
  ('dd8ee08d-b5d7-54a4-b5dd-c81024a95326','fa83069c-7a21-5f41-b8a7-e4d3b724a2e0',1,'D','τ = p - E (vector difference)'),
  ('3ad61843-fdf8-53e5-b905-9e97e73b1681','3cadf134-5424-5e42-b176-486ab2cdbc6f',1,'A','ΔΦ = E + ΔS'),
  ('6cd7a0c6-3518-5be9-9dea-5e8c042fb09c','3cadf134-5424-5e42-b176-486ab2cdbc6f',1,'B','ΔΦ = E × ΔS'),
  ('f627b2b2-2aca-5393-9c87-b240c19fe196','3cadf134-5424-5e42-b176-486ab2cdbc6f',1,'C','ΔΦ = E · ΔS = E ΔS cos θ'),
  ('517f8bfc-e197-5204-8a13-13b8147f1df2','3cadf134-5424-5e42-b176-486ab2cdbc6f',1,'D','ΔΦ = E / ΔS'),
  ('f53c7303-09dd-5b2d-9a0b-ba5d9674c57e','404891a2-31f8-5406-ae9f-555e338be9e2',1,'A','E = λ / (4πε₀r²)'),
  ('a3a0a4a1-504d-5527-9b32-ea7a4728a507','404891a2-31f8-5406-ae9f-555e338be9e2',1,'B','E = λ / (2πε₀r)'),
  ('7361876d-2d51-5c39-a545-2865f7ee6904','404891a2-31f8-5406-ae9f-555e338be9e2',1,'C','E = λ / (πε₀r)'),
  ('cd64175d-5807-554b-9226-6ae7f5f525bc','404891a2-31f8-5406-ae9f-555e338be9e2',1,'D','E = λ / (4πε₀r)'),
  ('0f56d2e7-dc06-553d-9ed4-a091f1f12c0b','29c059d7-c3e0-531e-8f97-2aedc31fb7dc',1,'A','E = σ / (4πε₀)'),
  ('fa34d454-b5ec-5a6c-a1ce-7fce2be7351f','29c059d7-c3e0-531e-8f97-2aedc31fb7dc',1,'B','E = σ / (2ε₀)'),
  ('06751113-88f0-5b1e-870a-572e6c7415f3','29c059d7-c3e0-531e-8f97-2aedc31fb7dc',1,'C','E = σ / ε₀'),
  ('d9ee0302-f79a-5bf8-9c32-2d9e83ccee05','29c059d7-c3e0-531e-8f97-2aedc31fb7dc',1,'D','E = 2σ / ε₀'),
  ('459cdcd5-6330-59b4-b851-be4d656f85d2','72e97548-ab35-5b5d-9bf1-423b89f3aedd',1,'A','E = q / (4πε₀R²)'),
  ('df19b3c6-c63c-533f-a13a-19167f9701ce','72e97548-ab35-5b5d-9bf1-423b89f3aedd',1,'B','E = q / (4πε₀r²)'),
  ('2dc52fd1-60f2-54ac-89d7-c7ae4d7401d1','72e97548-ab35-5b5d-9bf1-423b89f3aedd',1,'C','E = 0'),
  ('7da82bd4-01ac-5a5c-89cf-8d9facf280a9','72e97548-ab35-5b5d-9bf1-423b89f3aedd',1,'D','E = σ / ε₀'),
  ('51e6da07-91f0-5ccf-aa95-d5062082bef2','5d5392eb-8d8a-580b-acab-01d89dbcb152',1,'A','E = q / (4πε₀R²)'),
  ('0b210ae9-c6f1-52b4-b608-96ed54b34cb2','5d5392eb-8d8a-580b-acab-01d89dbcb152',1,'B','E = q / (4πε₀r²)'),
  ('5d745c7f-6324-57b3-9b09-180687ae71a3','5d5392eb-8d8a-580b-acab-01d89dbcb152',1,'C','E = σ / (2ε₀)'),
  ('6da3cc77-b0fe-5bc7-a661-77f34dfaa196','5d5392eb-8d8a-580b-acab-01d89dbcb152',1,'D','E = 0'),
  ('9a10b85b-9a17-5f93-8866-92569efb24ce','621ac44a-c4b3-52ff-a58e-d6cc8c0cb9a2',1,'A','Electrons orbiting the nucleus create a shielding effect that neutralizes the repulsion'),
  ('27ceaa52-6f5d-5f54-96be-70e6d8799515','621ac44a-c4b3-52ff-a58e-d6cc8c0cb9a2',1,'B','The gravitational attraction between protons overcomes the electrostatic repulsion'),
  ('2fa3c2ef-267f-55fe-bfa3-7daa08340f47','621ac44a-c4b3-52ff-a58e-d6cc8c0cb9a2',1,'C','A third fundamental force called the strong nuclear force holds them together within a range of approximately 10^-14 m'),
  ('ca977c5f-2541-5231-a09e-b0d86b1446bb','621ac44a-c4b3-52ff-a58e-d6cc8c0cb9a2',1,'D','The Coulomb force between protons becomes attractive at very short distances'),
  ('26811a09-e8fc-519c-aa94-d28f4b12778b','2fe38481-ec85-5874-bdc2-2e1a8c238a31',1,'A','Gravitational force acts instantaneously while Coulomb force has a time delay'),
  ('11f43d2c-b1a8-5ef8-9ae0-3742d9524246','2fe38481-ec85-5874-bdc2-2e1a8c238a31',1,'B','Gravitational force has only one sign (always attractive) while Coulomb force can be both attractive and repulsive, allowing cancellation of electric forces'),
  ('8e981473-859a-54da-a4d4-5f6889e74f32','2fe38481-ec85-5874-bdc2-2e1a8c238a31',1,'C','Gravitational force depends on mass while Coulomb force depends on charge, and mass is always positive'),
  ('300c0e49-f731-582b-95bd-82e7eeac7c36','2fe38481-ec85-5874-bdc2-2e1a8c238a31',1,'D','The gravitational constant G is larger than the Coulomb constant k'),
  ('46416a78-e7e8-5a60-8703-f14164f89daa','573e08b1-e402-5b77-96c7-0b5290b41888',1,'A','The unit of charge is defined using Coulomb''s law directly, making k exactly 9 x 10^9 N m^2 C^-2'),
  ('d8bb88d7-ef7c-5565-9c84-9b0681e62bce','573e08b1-e402-5b77-96c7-0b5290b41888',1,'B','The unit of current (ampere) is defined via its magnetic effect, and charge is defined as 1 C = 1 A s, making k approximately 9 x 10^9 N m^2 C^-2'),
  ('d84b44a7-321e-5a27-ba18-844c1864710d','573e08b1-e402-5b77-96c7-0b5290b41888',1,'C','The unit of charge is defined by the charge on a single electron, and k is calculated from that'),
  ('17021fc5-4099-58dc-934e-e6e9c0d19446','573e08b1-e402-5b77-96c7-0b5290b41888',1,'D','The unit of charge is defined by the force between two protons at 1 meter separation'),
  ('42ea2597-ef1e-5a7a-8673-b113b33cda1a','93a7a6ca-af77-54cc-b1a6-455caa983ed9',1,'A','Because charges can be positive or negative, making addition complicated'),
  ('63923b88-d709-5d72-b150-98aa70d9c4b7','93a7a6ca-af77-54cc-b1a6-455caa983ed9',1,'B','Because it is related to the fact that electric charge is a scalar quantity with no direction associated with it'),
  ('2588109a-fd54-5e59-b519-277420253c49','93a7a6ca-af77-54cc-b1a6-455caa983ed9',1,'C','Because charge is quantized and can only exist in discrete units'),
  ('c3197930-a8c0-57aa-aa3a-0241dfb8a681','93a7a6ca-af77-54cc-b1a6-455caa983ed9',1,'D','Because the total charge of a system is not always conserved'),
  ('053cbaf5-b20c-5992-b781-058fcfb93143','e1c38cd9-f7bf-527f-8d74-1c79f01f14bd',1,'A','Charge is a scalar only under rotation but changes under relative motion of reference frames'),
  ('9519d4be-ef69-5165-9c4f-623a3fa8f4c5','e1c38cd9-f7bf-527f-8d74-1c79f01f14bd',1,'B','Charge is invariant under both rotation and for frames of reference in relative motion, unlike kinetic energy which is not invariant under relative motion'),
  ('c8274420-7cf6-55d7-8a4d-844decea0c9c','e1c38cd9-f7bf-527f-8d74-1c79f01f14bd',1,'C','Charge behaves like kinetic energy, being a scalar under rotation but not invariant under relative motion'),
  ('13cb52e1-d0c7-5d2d-a857-0ab8497f0015','e1c38cd9-f7bf-527f-8d74-1c79f01f14bd',1,'D','Charge is a vector quantity that transforms under rotation but remains invariant under relative motion'),
  ('994ef55b-fc98-5e62-95fd-8d0e7fbdd88c','1a515bab-ea67-5090-97b1-430bd6822ac0',1,'A','Conservation of charge is a direct consequence of its scalar nature'),
  ('9bdd2620-6a90-5edb-b228-fcf795901a4b','1a515bab-ea67-5090-97b1-430bd6822ac0',1,'B','Conservation of total charge is independent of the scalar nature of charge; a quantity may be scalar but not conserved, or conserved but not scalar'),
  ('342227e7-3541-5f00-b245-d4ac4b3a2ca6','1a515bab-ea67-5090-97b1-430bd6822ac0',1,'C','Only scalar quantities can be conserved, making conservation dependent on the scalar nature'),
  ('31b34ebb-108f-5848-9f6a-7aa00a36ac62','1a515bab-ea67-5090-97b1-430bd6822ac0',1,'D','Vector quantities like angular momentum cannot be conserved, proving conservation depends on scalar nature'),
  ('70eaa9d4-a61d-5125-9791-92b36a11ad93','b6e010f5-e05d-57b3-a83e-d59154f0392c',1,'A','Both electric charge and mass are quantised in nature'),
  ('01c7d3ff-2d1b-5a7c-8dca-3a8eca9880dc','b6e010f5-e05d-57b3-a83e-d59154f0392c',1,'B','Electric charge is quantised but there is no analogous law for quantisation of mass'),
  ('005f4ff1-02f2-5eba-b9c7-e43c31a4355d','b6e010f5-e05d-57b3-a83e-d59154f0392c',1,'C','Mass is quantised but electric charge is not'),
  ('5f2a0adb-512c-5d6f-9c32-a379d551c48b','b6e010f5-e05d-57b3-a83e-d59154f0392c',1,'D','Neither electric charge nor mass is quantised; both are continuous quantities'),
  ('b192aa16-c73c-5571-aba7-419345f36e36','eeb9f0b6-87f9-5fb5-9eb6-a84c3040dcc1',1,'A','Because it only applies to electrostatic forces and not to other types of forces'),
  ('f905d2e1-6aa9-52d5-a715-7b15fab07a0f','eeb9f0b6-87f9-5fb5-9eb6-a84c3040dcc1',1,'B','Because it states that the force on one charge due to another is unaffected by other charges, AND there are no additional three-body, four-body, etc. forces that arise only when more than two charges are present'),
  ('34a6b294-30b6-57aa-aff3-3e9fb573d002','eeb9f0b6-87f9-5fb5-9eb6-a84c3040dcc1',1,'C','Because vector addition is a mathematical concept while superposition is a physical law'),
  ('d0d68803-45af-506e-9b1d-95fb2f478d32','eeb9f0b6-87f9-5fb5-9eb6-a84c3040dcc1',1,'D','Because superposition requires the charges to be stationary while vector addition does not'),
  ('acab17d3-7295-5296-b7ef-1503cb426fdf','8b82ca59-d106-5523-b3c7-40039818fa2d',1,'A','Electromagnetic force holds them together'),
  ('4ba5bb68-118a-598b-bd00-8baba28e65eb','8b82ca59-d106-5523-b3c7-40039818fa2d',1,'B','Gravitational force overcomes Coulomb repulsion'),
  ('e7062bda-fd09-5ad2-b8b7-af3e3f02b561','8b82ca59-d106-5523-b3c7-40039818fa2d',1,'C','Strong nuclear force binds them together over very short distances (~10^-14 m)'),
  ('5e6c7a23-45de-57d0-adc0-660f4ae5fb5e','8b82ca59-d106-5523-b3c7-40039818fa2d',1,'D','Quantum mechanics prevents their separation'),
  ('2b1a4288-d908-5ff6-9a09-9e5d01bd983b','5f69bb0f-1853-5d2b-bb43-cfeff15d25c8',1,'A','Gravitational force has a longer range than Coulomb force'),
  ('0449b719-40d1-561c-8161-d1436f245416','5f69bb0f-1853-5d2b-bb43-cfeff15d25c8',1,'B','Coulomb force can be both attractive and repulsive, allowing cancellation, while gravity is always attractive'),
  ('c9975482-b366-53b3-9e2c-89ef50427a75','5f69bb0f-1853-5d2b-bb43-cfeff15d25c8',1,'C','Gravitational force is stronger at large distances'),
  ('a8eef478-783e-5168-8f22-2357b82a1d06','5f69bb0f-1853-5d2b-bb43-cfeff15d25c8',1,'D','Coulomb force depends on mass while gravity depends on charge'),
  ('6a6422b6-5e17-51a4-83b3-cc08c7739abc','968106a3-3312-5039-8e0f-b7d6d7f91303',1,'A','Charge is defined via Coulombs law directly, making k arbitrary'),
  ('3cabfe7d-68eb-552b-a750-b71376034b6a','968106a3-3312-5039-8e0f-b7d6d7f91303',1,'B','Unit of current (A) is defined via magnetic effect (Amperes law), and charge is defined as 1 C = 1 A s, making k approximately 9 x 10^9 N m^2 C^-2'),
  ('0288b845-6da0-5618-af66-95a1d24de708','968106a3-3312-5039-8e0f-b7d6d7f91303',1,'C','k is chosen to be exactly 1 for simplicity'),
  ('5a4f2175-3462-5fc8-85cb-99dcac5a4583','968106a3-3312-5039-8e0f-b7d6d7f91303',1,'D','Charge is defined through gravitational force measurements'),
  ('f76b7eaf-e0ea-5898-a6ce-9bca14fec92e','725cf4e8-380d-5b95-8e3c-d28e02e5b6f0',1,'A','Because electric forces are inherently weaker than magnetic forces'),
  ('83a5c890-0e83-540d-a35a-2be4c26b9192','725cf4e8-380d-5b95-8e3c-d28e02e5b6f0',1,'B','Because the unit of charge is defined in terms of magnetic forces (on current-carrying wires), which are generally much weaker than electric forces'),
  ('f184b482-e9d1-517a-b46b-7f8508104cbd','725cf4e8-380d-5b95-8e3c-d28e02e5b6f0',1,'C','Because 1 ampere is too small for magnetic measurements'),
  ('8de22130-9555-5f70-969d-82b59acce559','725cf4e8-380d-5b95-8e3c-d28e02e5b6f0',1,'D','Because Coulombs constant k is very small'),
  ('2e3ba829-96be-5a68-9070-eaf1bbf583f0','e3ca1199-18d3-58f6-9a5f-204ea95b3ef5',1,'A','Because charge can be both positive and negative'),
  ('af8eac81-941a-5d78-b098-78a97b1c5296','e3ca1199-18d3-58f6-9a5f-204ea95b3ef5',1,'B','Because it is related to the fact that charge is a scalar with no direction associated with it'),
  ('6e3ab83b-efe4-52ed-9317-76bfa848ac17','e3ca1199-18d3-58f6-9a5f-204ea95b3ef5',1,'C','Because charge is always conserved'),
  ('c85aa091-ea23-5b94-a345-64a12028f010','e3ca1199-18d3-58f6-9a5f-204ea95b3ef5',1,'D','Because charge is quantized'),
  ('16ffeba1-e05a-5271-82a6-264323ce91d0','0858ab90-d8d6-5bb0-9dfb-694aa1ed5344',1,'A','Charge changes sign when reference frames move'),
  ('f0c91d25-350b-57a4-8284-d345bbb28039','0858ab90-d8d6-5bb0-9dfb-694aa1ed5344',1,'B','Charge is invariant for frames of reference in relative motion, unlike kinetic energy which is not invariant'),
  ('cfb91fc8-99ab-5296-ad0d-6ef28fe668bf','0858ab90-d8d6-5bb0-9dfb-694aa1ed5344',1,'C','Charge increases with relative velocity'),
  ('931fe973-4932-5497-a744-57ffcb2ec2a8','0858ab90-d8d6-5bb0-9dfb-694aa1ed5344',1,'D','Charge decreases with relative velocity'),
  ('7fb3e8d1-933f-5462-9ea9-1cfe322e9d4e','91c5df6e-7075-5d91-85c9-8bf6ad2dca19',1,'A','The charge on a neutron'),
  ('9935c501-4815-5304-9a24-910062d10254','91c5df6e-7075-5d91-85c9-8bf6ad2dca19',1,'B','The charge on an electron or proton'),
  ('8039a85d-26e2-5856-8a4a-ff99a7bb2b7c','91c5df6e-7075-5d91-85c9-8bf6ad2dca19',1,'C','The charge on an alpha particle'),
  ('78cfe523-1cad-5948-8c12-636b9bba8efe','91c5df6e-7075-5d91-85c9-8bf6ad2dca19',1,'D','The charge on a photon'),
  ('353ef976-4f4e-5490-bdc5-11798c0f62e0','50995f0a-4146-538b-9a91-fd23dedeba03',1,'A','q = n^2 e'),
  ('39e18db6-4bcc-5edd-810a-d8a41ce280dd','50995f0a-4146-538b-9a91-fd23dedeba03',1,'B','q = ne where n is any integer'),
  ('e2893fc4-ce27-51ad-9cdf-1d8877177abb','50995f0a-4146-538b-9a91-fd23dedeba03',1,'C','q = e/n where n is any integer'),
